# Git branching

For every prompt that changes the repo, do the work on a branch. Do not commit feature work directly to `master`.

## Start

1. Check `git status` and the current branch before editing.
2. If you are on `master` (or about to start a new feature/fix), create and switch to a branch from current `master`:
   - `feature/<short-topic>` for new behavior
   - `fix/<short-topic>` for bug fixes
3. Keep one concern per branch. Do not pile unrelated features onto an existing branch.

```text
git checkout master
git checkout -b feature/project-sidebar
```

## Finish

Merge back to `master` only when **all** of these are true:

- The requested feature or fix is fully implemented
- It has been tested (tests, API checks, and/or UI verification as the work requires)
- The working tree for that feature is ready to commit

Then:

```text
git checkout master
git merge --no-ff feature/project-sidebar
```

Always use `--no-ff` when merging into `master` so the feature branch remains a visible merge commit. Do not merge half-finished work. Never force-push `master`. Do not delete or push branches unless the user asks.

If the user only asked a question and no code changes are needed, do not create a branch.
